= Planning (artificial intelligence) =

== Overview ==
In artificial intelligence, '''planning''' is the computational problem of selecting or synthesizing actions that can move an agent or system from an initial situation toward a goal under a model of actions and environment dynamics.

Automated planning is one of the classical areas of AI.

== Planning problem ==
A planning problem commonly specifies:

* an initial state or belief state;
* available actions;
* action preconditions or applicability;
* action effects or transition dynamics;
* goals or objectives.

A planner searches or reasons over possible action sequences, policies, or partial plans.

== Planning and search ==
Planning often uses search, but:

<pre>
planning ≠ search by definition
</pre>

Planning systems can use heuristic search, constraint solving, satisfiability, dynamic programming, hierarchical decomposition, symbolic reasoning, or other methods.

== Planning and acting ==
A plan is not the same thing as execution.

Ghallab, Nau, and Traverso emphasize that acting in real environments can require monitoring, feedback, replanning, and continual deliberation.

Thus:

<pre>
plan synthesis ≠ plan execution
</pre>

== Classical and nonclassical planning ==
Classical planning often assumes a deterministic, fully observable, static environment with known actions.

Other planning frameworks handle uncertainty, partial observability, time, resources, multiple agents, continuous dynamics, or changing environments.

== LLM-agent planning ==
Modern AI-agent systems often use language models to decompose tasks or produce textual action sequences.

Those techniques may implement useful planning behavior, but they should not replace the broader automated-planning concept.

A generated chain of text is not automatically a valid plan.

